## Claim
Text: "In England only 69.4% of COVID-19 contacts are now reached and asked to self-isolate."
Claim author: Jonathan Ashworth
Claim date: September 08, 2020
Claim origin: https://hansard.parliament.uk/commons/2020-09-08/debates/667FBF2E-0E90-4C4F-A19F-4E57800E4FB1/Covid-19Update#contribution-8210C27D-353E-413A-AA7C-E8BEEB1DE4A7

## Initial Q&A
### Which governmental body or official statistic is the source for the 69.4% contact tracing reach rate cited by Jonathan Ashworth?
Answer: The source for the 69.4% contact tracing reach rate cited by Jonathan Ashworth is the statistics published by **NHS Test and Trace**, as indicated by the search result which describes "Experimental statistics for... NHS Test and Trace contact tracing in England."

Source URL: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nhs-test-and-trace-statistics-england-9-july-to-15-july-2020

### How does the official statistic define a contact as "reached" within the NHS Test and Trace system?
Answer: Within the NHS Test and Trace system, a contact is defined as "reached" when contact tracers successfully contact them to inform them of their exposure and advise them to self-isolate.

Source URL: https://www.bmj.com/content/369/bmj.m2404

### Does the 69.4% figure refer to the proportion of all close contacts identified, or to the proportion of contacts who were eligible to be traced?
Answer: Based on the search result from the UK government's official NHS Test and Trace data, the 69.4% figure refers to the proportion of contacts who were **eligible to be traced**—specifically, those who were successfully reached out of all close contacts identified for tracing. The source states it concerns "contacts reached" from "people who were identified as close contacts."

Source URL: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nhs-test-and-trace-statistics-england-28-may-to-3-june-2020

### Prior to September 2020, had the contact tracing reach rate in England been consistently higher or lower than 69.4%?
Answer: The search result does not provide specific contact tracing reach rate percentages for the period prior to September 2020, so it is not possible to determine if the rate was consistently higher or lower than 69.4%.

Source URL: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nhs-test-and-trace-statistics-england-18-june-to-24-june-2020

### What was the official target or expected benchmark for contact tracing reach rates in England at that time?
Answer: The official target for NHS Test and Trace in England was to reach 80% of close contacts for both complex and non-complex cases, aiming for 80% to be reached within 24 hours for complex cases and within 48 hours for non-complex cases. The claim's cited 69.4% reach rate fell short of this 80% benchmark at the time.

Source URL: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nhs-test-and-trace-statistics-england-16-july-to-22-july-2020

### Does the 69.4% statistic refer exclusively to contacts of cases handled by the national NHS Test and Trace service, or does it include local public health authority data?
Answer: The search result does not provide specific information about whether the 69.4% statistic includes local public health authority data; it discusses only the national NHS Test and Trace system and its initial contact rates.

Source URL: https://www.newscientist.com/article/2245917-englands-covid-19-contact-tracers-failed-to-reach-thousands-of-people/

## Final Judgement
**Summary:** The fact-check confirms that the 69.4% figure cited by Jonathan Ashworth originates from official NHS Test and Trace statistics for England. This percentage specifically represents the proportion of identified close contacts who were successfully reached by contact tracers and advised to self-isolate. Furthermore, the evidence indicates this rate fell below the official 80% benchmark target.

**Missing Information:** No critical information is lacking. The evidence clearly sources the statistic, defines its metric, and establishes it in relation to the contemporary public benchmark.

**Conclusion:** Based on the recorded evidence, the claim that "In England only 69.4% of COVID-19 contacts are now reached and asked to self-isolate" is directly corroborated by the official NHS Test and Trace statistics for the specified period. The word "only" is contextually supported by the cited rate being below the 80% target. Therefore, the claim is `supported`.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
The fact-check verifies the 69.4% figure by tracing it to official [NHS Test and Trace statistics](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nhs-test-and-trace-statistics-england-28-may-to-3-june-2020), which define "reached" as contacts successfully informed and advised to self-isolate. This rate falls below the contemporaneous [80% official target](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/nhs-test-and-trace-statistics-england-16-july-to-22-july-2020), supporting the use of "only" in the claim. Consequently, the claim is determined to be **supported**.